{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Foundations of CBI and Design Tensions", "title": "Classroom Context Variables", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting classroom context variables to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "01", "section": "05", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/01/04"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_1_5_classroom_context_variables_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_5_classroom_context_variables_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_5_classroom_context_variables_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_1_5_classroom_context_variables_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 5, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Classroom Context Variables\n\n![Classroom Context Variables textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/01-05-classroom-context-variables.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA research finding or a vendor case study always comes attached to a context, but the context is usually printed in small type while the headline number is in bold. \"Raised participation 30 percent\" travels; \"in classes of twelve with one-to-one laptops and no multilingual learners\" gets left behind. When a teacher imports the number without the conditions, she is not borrowing evidence; she is borrowing a result that may not exist in her room.\n\nThis section matters because the difference between a finding that transfers and one that does not is a finite list of context variables you can actually write down and compare. Class size, device ratio, language profile, accommodations, home connectivity, the platform's interaction model. The professional move is not to dismiss outside evidence and not to swallow it whole, but to lay your context next to the study's context, variable by variable, and decide where the gap is large enough to break the claim.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher reads that a discussion-bot \"raised participation by 30 percent in a published study\" and wants to roll it out to all her sections next week. The number is real, but it was produced under conditions she has not examined. Her own room has 34 students, a cart of 10 shared tablets, 9 multilingual learners, and several students without reliable home internet. The question is not whether the 30 percent happened, but whether the conditions that produced it exist in her classroom.\n\nThe record that matters here is not \"a study supports this tool.\" It is a side-by-side comparison of the study's context variables and her own, with a decision that follows from where they diverge.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA context variable is any classroom condition that can change whether an instructional result reproduces. The core skill is treating a borrowed finding as conditional, not universal: the claim is \"this tool produced this effect under these conditions,\" and your job is to check whether the conditions hold for you.\n\nThe variables that most often break transfer fall into four families:\n\n- **Scale.** Class size and the student-to-device ratio. A discussion tool that thrives at 12 students with 1:1 devices behaves differently at 34 students sharing 10 tablets.\n- **Learner composition.** Number of multilingual learners, students with IEP or 504 accommodations, reading-level spread. A text-only, fast-typing interaction model advantages some compositions and disadvantages others.\n- **Access conditions.** Home connectivity, device ownership, whether the activity assumes work outside class.\n- **Interaction model fit.** Whether the tool's mode of engagement (typing speed, screen time, real-time response) matches how this group can actually participate.\n\nThe disciplined comparison is variable by variable, not impression by impression. Where a variable matches, the borrowed evidence carries weight; where it diverges sharply, the evidence is suspended until you test it locally. Critical edtech studies is the lens for who a context mismatch excludes; datafication is the lens for whether the tool's model was built for learners like yours; [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) keeps the comparison tied to the actual content goal rather than novelty.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A study reports a large effect size under conditions you do not share. What does the large effect tell you about transfer to your room?\" explanation=\"A large effect under conditions you do not share is a large effect you may never see. Effect size measures how strong the result was in the study's context; it says nothing about whether the load-bearing context variables hold for you. Transfer is decided by graded variable-by-variable comparison, not by the magnitude of the original effect.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A bigger effect guarantees stronger transfer\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing about transfer; it is a strong effect you may never see\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Effect size replaces the need to compare contexts\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It means the tool is universally effective\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe study reported its 30 percent result with 12 students, a 1:1 device ratio, and 0 multilingual learners. Set your own room's variables and watch the divergence on each load-bearing dimension. The wider the gap, the more the borrowed result must be suspended and tested locally.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Context divergence: the discussion-bot 30-percent study vs. your room\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"my_size\",\"label\":\"Your class size\",\"min\":8,\"max\":40,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"students\",\"default\":34},{\"name\":\"my_devices\",\"label\":\"Your shared devices\",\"min\":4,\"max\":40,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"devices\",\"default\":10},{\"name\":\"my_ml\",\"label\":\"Your multilingual learners\",\"min\":0,\"max\":20,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"students\",\"default\":9}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Your student-to-device ratio\",\"unit\":\"per device\",\"expr\":\"my_size / my_devices\"},{\"label\":\"Class-size gap vs. study (12)\",\"unit\":\"students\",\"expr\":\"my_size - 12\"},{\"label\":\"Multilingual gap vs. study (0)\",\"unit\":\"students\",\"expr\":\"my_ml\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"my_size\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nScenario: the teacher from the opening case decides whether the discussion-bot's 30 percent result applies to her. The judgment turns on whether her classroom context matches the study's. Walk it in four numbered moves.\n\n**Before (context-blind framing).** Her note reads:\n\n> The study shows the discussion bot raises participation 30 percent, so I will roll it out to all my sections next week.\n\n1. **Pull the study's context variables.** Reported setting: 12 students per class, 1:1 laptops, 0 multilingual learners flagged, all students with home broadband.\n2. **Pull her own context variables.** Her setting: 34 students, 10 shared tablets on a cart, 9 multilingual learners, 7 students with IEP/504 accommodations, about a third without reliable home internet.\n3. **Compare variable by variable.** Class size 12 vs. 34. Device ratio 1:1 vs. roughly 1:3.4. Language profile 0 vs. 9 multilingual. The bot's text-only, fast-typing interaction model fits the study's room but not hers.\n4. **Decide using the mismatch.** The 30 percent figure does not transfer. She will pilot the bot with one 34-student section, on a rotation so the device ratio is manageable, with a voice-input fallback for multilingual learners, and measure participation in HER room before any wider rollout.\n\n**After (context-aware framing).** The revised note reads:\n\n> The 30 percent study ran with 12 students, 1:1 devices, and no multilingual learners. My section has 34 students, a 1:3.4 device ratio, and 9 multilingual learners. I am not assuming the result transfers. I will pilot one section with device rotation and a voice-input fallback, then check my own participation data before scaling.\n\nThe decision is defensible because the context variables that govern transfer were listed and compared, so the borrowed evidence is tested against this room rather than assumed to apply.\n\nWork the rollout decision as a branching judgment. At each branch, decide whether the borrowed result has earned the right to scale in your room.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Should the 30-percent discussion-bot roll out next week?\",\"start\":\"c1\",\"nodes\":{\"c1\":{\"prompt\":\"You have a published 30-percent participation gain and a deadline. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Roll it out to all sections; the study is published and the number is real\",\"next\":\"cBlind\",\"feedback\":\"The number is real, but you imported the result without its conditions. The 30 percent was produced under a context you have not examined.\"},{\"label\":\"Pull the context variables the study reported and lay yours beside them\",\"next\":\"cCompare\",\"feedback\":\"Right move. Treat the finding as conditional: this effect, under these conditions. Now check whether the conditions hold.\"}]},\"cBlind\":{\"prompt\":\"Three weeks in, participation has not moved and your multilingual learners are barely posting. What went wrong?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The study was flawed\",\"next\":\"cLoss\",\"feedback\":\"More likely the load-bearing variables (class size, device ratio, language profile) never matched your room.\"},{\"label\":\"The text-only, fast-typing model never fit a 34-student, 1:3.4-device, 9-multilingual class\",\"next\":\"cWin\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly the mismatch a variable-by-variable comparison would have caught before rollout.\"}]},\"cCompare\":{\"prompt\":\"Study: 12 students, 1:1 laptops, 0 multilingual. You: 34 students, 10 shared tablets, 9 multilingual. The class-size, device-ratio, and language variables all diverge. What follows?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Scale anyway; the gaps are minor\",\"next\":\"cLoss\",\"feedback\":\"All three diverging variables are load-bearing for a text-only, fast-typing tool. Scaling on faith ignores the mismatch.\"},{\"label\":\"Suspend the borrowed result and pilot one section with device rotation and a voice-input fallback\",\"next\":\"cWin\",\"feedback\":\"You suspended the claim where variables broke and planned a local test that protects multilingual learners.\"}]},\"cWin\":{\"prompt\":\"You measured participation in YOUR room before scaling, with safeguards for the diverging variables.\",\"feedback\":\"This is graded transfer: keep the evidence where variables match, test it where they break.\",\"choices\":[]},\"cLoss\":{\"prompt\":\"You scaled a result whose conditions you did not share, and a concentrated group paid for the mismatch.\",\"feedback\":\"Revisit: name the reported class size, device ratio, and language profile, then decide where the gap is large enough to break the claim.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore a borrowed result can justify a tool, the value conflict in your room comes first, the affordance second, the policy boundary third, and the context comparison underwrites all three. A tool choice is justified only when it protects students while improving a specific learning activity in the conditions you actually teach in.\n\nA defensible context judgment survives three quick tests:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Does the study's outcome actually match the goal you have for your students, or just sound impressive?\n2. **Evidence check:** Did you pull the study's context variables and lay them next to yours, variable by variable?\n3. **Revision check:** Where the contexts diverge, did you suspend the claim and plan a local pilot rather than scale on faith?\n\nIf you cannot name the study's class size, device ratio, and learner profile, you cannot yet say the result transfers.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most frequent error is treating \"engaging in the study\" as \"engaging for my students,\" which skips the composition and access variables entirely. A second is reading a large effect size as strong transfer evidence; a large effect under conditions you do not share is a large effect you may never see. A third is the opposite overcorrection, dismissing all outside evidence because contexts always differ; the skill is graded comparison, keeping the evidence where variables match and suspending it where they break.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the comparison to a new claim: a vendor says its math-fluency game \"improved scores in a pilot of 200 students.\" A teacher is tempted to adopt it for her two sections.\n\nPull the pilot's context. The fine print: suburban district, 1:1 Chromebooks, daily 20-minute home practice assigned and completed, students screened to grade level. Pull her context: an urban section with shared devices, no reliable way to assign home practice, and a reading-level spread of three grades, several below the game's text demands.\n\nCompare variable by variable. The 20-minute daily home-practice assumption is load-bearing for the pilot's result, and it simply does not hold for her students without home devices; the reading-level mismatch means her struggling readers will spend the game decoding instructions rather than practicing math. Two large variables diverge.\n\nDecide from the mismatch. She does not adopt on the vendor's number. She runs a two-week in-class-only pilot with audio instructions enabled, measures fluency in her own sections, and compares before deciding. The walkthrough shows the same variable-by-variable discipline applied to a vendor pilot rather than a journal study, with the divergent variables driving a local test instead of a leap.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: each **context variable** on the left, **study value versus my value** on the right. The variables where the two columns differ most are your transfer risks; circle them. For this topic, the tell that you are reading well is that you can name at least one variable in the study's fine print that the headline omitted.\n\nA strong reader of context variables can answer four questions:\n\n1. What scale, composition, and access conditions produced the original result?\n2. Which of those conditions does my classroom not share?\n3. Is the diverging variable load-bearing for the effect, or incidental?\n4. Where the contexts break, what local pilot would test the claim in my room?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when the study's context variables and your own are listed side by side and the decision follows from the specific divergences, not from the headline number. An artifact that cites the 30 percent without the conditions has imported a result it cannot support; mark that gap and ask BigAL to compare a context-blind adoption against a context-matched one rather than for a general explanation.\n\nA useful closing line sounds like: \"The study ran at 12 students with 1:1 devices and no multilingual learners; my section diverges on all three, so I am piloting locally with a voice fallback before scaling.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the discussion-bot decision into a small honors seminar of 11 students, all native speakers, each with a laptop. The teacher's context now nearly matches the study's. The same variable-by-variable comparison that blocked adoption in the 34-student room may now support it, because the load-bearing variables align. If your method only ever produces \"do not adopt,\" you learned a reflex; if it produces \"adopt here, suspend there\" depending on the variables, you understood context.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/01-05-classroom-context-variables-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"01.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 01.05. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how context-variable comparison connects to evidence-based revision and the transfer anchors.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions\", \"cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_classroom_context_variables_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables", "stem": "A teacher read that a discussion-bot tool 'raised engagement in a study' and plans to adopt it. Which classroom context variable, if unexamined, would most undermine that evidence transferring to her room?", "options": ["The font size used in the bot's interface.", "The study's classes were 12 students with 1:1 devices, while her class is 34 students sharing a cart of 10 tablets.", "The color of the study's cover page.", "Whether the study was published recently."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Context variables such as class size and device ratio govern whether evidence from one setting transfers. A 12-student 1:1 study tells you little about a 34-student shared-device room.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_evidence_transfers_context_free", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_classroom_context_variables_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables", "stem": "A teacher lists her context as 'a normal high school class.' A mentor says this description is too thin to support a tool decision. Which set of variables would make the context decision-relevant?", "options": ["The school mascot, the bell schedule, and the room number.", "Class size, device-to-student ratio, proportion of multilingual learners, students with IEPs/504 plans, and reliable home-internet access.", "The teacher's years of experience only.", "The brand of the classroom projector."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Decision-relevant context variables are the ones that change whether a tool works and for whom: class size, device ratio, language profile, accommodations, and connectivity.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_context_as_vague_label", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_classroom_context_variables_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "A teacher asks an AI to 'recommend a vocabulary tool for my class.' The AI gives a confident recommendation without asking anything. What context-aware move keeps the decision defensible?", "options": ["Accept the recommendation; the AI is trained on many classrooms.", "Supply the AI the specific context variables (class size, language profile, device access) and ask it to flag where the recommendation might fail for her room, then judge the fit.", "Ask the AI to pick for every class in the school at once.", "Reject all AI recommendations as context-blind."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A context-free recommendation cannot account for the variables that decide fit. The defensible move is to feed the AI the actual context and use it to surface where the tool might fail, not to outsource the judgment.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_ai_recommends_context_free", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_classroom_context_variables_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables", "stem": "A tool worked well in a teacher's 1st-period class but failed in her 5th-period class with the same lesson. She concludes 'the tool is unreliable.' What is the more defensible reading?", "options": ["The tool is simply broken and should be discarded.", "Context variables differ across the two periods (for example more multilingual learners or a co-teaching schedule in 5th), so the failure is a fit problem to diagnose, not proof the tool is universally bad.", "5th-period students are less capable.", "The lesson should never be reused across periods."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Differing outcomes across sections usually signal differing context variables, not a defective tool. The defensible move is to identify which variable changed and adjust, not to generalize 'unreliable.'", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_outcome_generalized_across_contexts", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_classroom_context_variables", "evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "9c4e873b4dd1d66398094df809668d1f455b39056d579ef52c839214ab54021d", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_evidence_transfers_context_free", "pattern": "evidence_transfers_regardless_of_context", "description": "Assuming evidence that a tool worked in one study or classroom transfers to any other setting regardless of context variables", "trigger": "learner adopts a tool on study evidence without checking whether the study's context matches their own", "feedback": "Compare the context variables. Class size, device ratio, and language profile govern transfer; evidence from a different setting may not carry over.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_context_as_vague_label", "pattern": "context_described_too_thinly", "description": "Describing classroom context with vague labels instead of the decision-relevant variables", "trigger": "learner characterizes the room as 'a normal class' with no specific variables", "feedback": "Replace the label with decision-relevant variables: class size, device ratio, multilingual proportion, accommodations, and connectivity.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_ai_recommends_context_free", "pattern": "ai_recommends_without_context", "description": "Accepting an AI tool recommendation that was made without knowing the classroom's context variables", "trigger": "learner takes a context-free AI recommendation as a fit judgment for their specific room", "feedback": "Feed the AI your actual context variables and ask it where the tool might fail for your room, then judge the fit yourself.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_5_outcome_generalized_across_contexts", "pattern": "outcome_generalized_ignoring_variables", "description": "Generalizing a tool as good or bad from one section's outcome without diagnosing which context variable changed", "trigger": "learner declares a tool reliable or unreliable based on differing results across periods", "feedback": "Differing results across sections usually mean differing context variables. Identify which variable changed before generalizing about the tool.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}